          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            To recall, the subject of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            studies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            between them:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            only up to continuous
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformations"} deformations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Higher+Structures+in+Mathematics+and+Physics"} Higher Structures in Mathematics and Physics
          %p
            In order to study
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            – then called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            – a useful strategy is to assign
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
            data to them which may be transferred along
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            but is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            under
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariants"} homotopy invariants
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p
            A basic example of such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariant"} homotopy invariant
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            which classify formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+linear+combinations"} formal linear combinations
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chains"}> singular chains
            ” in a topological space, essentially a simple algebraic way to detect which kind of “holes” there are in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %p
            But there are more interesting and richer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariants"} homotopy invariants
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
            is the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"}> generalized cohomology theory
            ” called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            one detects properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            by regarding
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            over them. A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            over a topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an assignment of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_x
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"}> fiber
            ” over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            varies in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TangentSpaceToSphere.png", :width => "250"}/
            %blockquote
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/vector+bundle#Hatcher"} Hatcher
          %p
            For example if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vectors"} tangent vectors
            called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            at that point. The collection of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+spaces"} tangent spaces
            forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
          %p
            Just as two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            may be combined in their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            may be combined by point-wise direct sum of their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            over a fixed topological space form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
            (in fact a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            is to turn this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
            of vector bundles into an actual (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"}> abelian
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            by adjoining
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverses"} inverses
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            construction, after its inventor. Applied to the semi-group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            group of the underlying topological space. Here the “K” refers to the passage to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"}> isomorphism
            ) classes (German:
            = succeed ")." do
              %em Klassen
          .un_remark{:style => "border:solid #0000cc;background: #add8e6;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %p
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} Topological K-theory
                studies
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
                over
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                together with their formal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverses"} inverses
                with respect to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum+of+vector+bundles"} direct sum of vector bundles
              \.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p We now say some of this again, at a slightly more technical level.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Recall that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            then a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \to X
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            which vary over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a controlled way. Explicitly this means that there exits an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            (the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank+of+a+vector+bundle"} rank
            of the vector bundle) and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \times k^n \to V|_{U_i}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            which is fiberwise a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
          %p
            Vector bundles are of central interest in large parts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theory"} cobordism theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect(X)_{/\sim}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of vector bundles over a given space is in general hard to analyze. One reason for this is that these are classified in degree-1
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+group"}> nonabelian
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(n,k)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
          %p
            To that end, observe that all natural operations on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            generalize to vector bundles by applying them
            = succeed "-wise." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum+of+vector+bundles"} direct sum of vector bundles
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitney+sum"} Whitney sum
            operation. This operation gives the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect(X)_{/\sim}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of vector bundles the structure of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"}> monoid
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo ∼
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Vect(X)_{/\sim},\oplus)
          %p
            Now as under direct sum, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            of vector spaces adds, similarly under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum+of+vector+bundles"} direct sum of vector bundles
            their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank"} rank
            adds. Hence in analogy to how one passes from the additive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"}> monoid
            ) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            to the addtitive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            by adjoining formal additive inverses, so one may adjoin formal additive inverses to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo ∼
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Vect(X)_{/\sim},\oplus)
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V_+,V_-)
            of vector bundles (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+vector+bundles"}> virtual vector bundles
            ”), and then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotienting
            out the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (V_+, V_-) \sim (V_+ \oplus W , V_- \oplus W)
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \in Vect(X)_{/\sim}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            with group operation given on representatives by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [V_+, V_-] \oplus [V'_+, V'_-] \coloneqq (V_+ \oplus V'_+, V_- \oplus V'_-)
          %p
            and with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [V_+,V_-]
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \-[V_+, V_-] = [V_-, V_+]
                  \\,.
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            obtained from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo ∼
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Vect(X)_{/\sim}, \oplus)
            is denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X)
            and often called
            %em the K-theory
            of the space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
            = succeed "," do
              %em Klasse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"}> isomorphism classes
            ) of vector bundles.
          %p
            This simple construction turns out to yield remarkably useful groups of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariants"} invariants
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            have fairly elementary proofs in terms of topolgical K-theory, for instance
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the proof of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
            %li
              %p
                the solution of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant+one"} Hopf invariant one
                problem (
                %a{:href => "#AdamsAtiyah66"}> Adams-Atiyah 66
                ),
          %p
            These proofs make use of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+operations"} Adams operations
            on K-theory, which are reflections of the fact that with every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            there is also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+power"} exterior
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+bundles"} tensor powers
          %p
            One defines the “higher” K-groups of a topological space to be those of its higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspensions"} suspensions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K^{-n}(X) = K(\Sigma^n X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto K^\bullet(X)
            turns out to share many properties of the assignment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            groups
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto H^n(X,\mathbb{Z})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory. As such it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
            by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{C}
            this is called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{R}
            this is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO"} KO
            = succeed "-theory.)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KR"} KR
          %p
            One of the basic facts about topological K-theory, rather unexpected from the definition, is that these higher K-groups repeat
            %em periodically
            in the degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{R}
            the periodicity is 8, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{C}
            it is 2. This is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity"} Bott periodicity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebras"} Clifford algebras
          %p
            It turns out that an important source of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+vector+bundles"} virtual vector bundles
            representing classes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            are index bundles: Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \to B
            called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+bundle"} spin bundle
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+of+a+Dirac+operator"} index of a Dirac operator
            is the formal difference of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            by its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ker
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi coker
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [ker D, coker D]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_x
            of Dirac operators/Fredholm operators, parameterized by some topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X)
          %p
            It is via this construction that topological K-theory connects to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+geometry"} spin geometry
            (see e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+K-theory"} Karoubi K-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
          %p
            As the terminology indicates, both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+geometry"} spin geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            originate in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+quantization"}> spin^c quantization
            ”) in the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            (where it models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge"} D-brane charge
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"} spectral triples
            (see below).
          %p
            All these geometric constructions have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebraic
            incarnation: by the topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre-Swan+theorem"} Serre-Swan theorem
            then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            of finite rank are equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            on the base space. Using this relation one may express K-theory classes entirely operator algebraically, this is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operators"} Dirac operators
            are generalized to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operators"} Fredholm operators
          %p
            There are more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            than arising as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+functions"} algebras of functions
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %em
              \-algebras. One may think of these as defining
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+geometry"} non-commutative geometry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-theory
              immediately generalizes to this situation (see also at
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            remembers only the underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+structure"} Riemannian structure
            by passing from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+modules"} Fredholm modules
            to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"}> spectral triples
            ”. This may for instance be used to algebraically encode the spin physics underlying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-theory
            plays a crucial role in this.
          %h2#ordinary_cohomology
            %strong Ordinary cohomology
          %h3#idea_2 Idea
          %p
            We are concerned with assigning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            that tell us something about the nature of these spaces, about their “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"}> homotopy type
            ”. Before we come to K-theory, the simplest such assignment is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chain+homology"} singular chain homology
            it uses a basic tool of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            to compute these group:
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
          %p
            Consider an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \colon [0,1] \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            is the two endpoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(0) \colon \ast \to X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo *
                    %mi to
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(1) \colon \ast to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+sum"} formal difference
            and call this the
            %em oriented boundary
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial \sigma \;\coloneqq\; \sigma(1) - \sigma(0)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This suggests that we also allow for signed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+sums"} formal sums
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{i}{\sum} a_i \sigma_i
            of intervals in the first place.{ If all these intervals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_i
            touch each other at their endpoints, then this looks like a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain"} chain
            of intervals, a
            = succeed "," do
              %em 1-dimensional chain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+sum"} formal sum
            is called a
            = succeed "," do
              %em 1-chain
            = succeed "." do
              %em 0-chain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial
            linearly from intervals to 1-chains. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+group"} quotient group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mfrac
                    %mtext 0-chains
                    %mtext boundaries of 1-chains
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_0(X) \coloneqq \frac{\text{0-chains}}{\text{boundaries of 1-chains}}
          %p
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in degree 0. Clearly, it is measure for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial
            on all of these. It satisfies the fundamental identity that
            = succeed "," do
              %em The boundary of a boundary vanishes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial \circ \partial = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            One says that the chains equipped with this nilpotent boundary operation form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+group"} quotient group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mtext -chains that are cycles
                    %mrow
                      %mtext boundaries of
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mtext -chains
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_n(X) \coloneqq \frac{n\text{-chains that are cycles}}{\text{boundaries of}\; n+1\text{-chains}}
          %p
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The ordinary homólogy groups associated with a topological space are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariants"} homotopy invariants
            that are useful for analyzing, recognizing and and distinguishing topological spaces.
          %h3#HomotopyType Homotopy type of topological spaces
          %p
            This section reviews some basic notions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong topological space
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              equipped with a set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            %ol
              %li
                finite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
              %li
                arbitrary
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              with its standard notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              given by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi n
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^n \subset \mathbb{R}^n
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \hookrightarrow X
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
              of sets and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \subset X\}_{i \in I}
              a topology on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \cap Y \subset Y\}_{i \in I}
          #TopologicalSimplex.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %strong
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/simplex#TopologicalSimplex"} topological n-simplex
              is, up to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              whose underlying set is the subset
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi i
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta^n \coloneqq
                    \\{
                    \\vec x \in \mathbb{R}^{n+1}
                    |
                    \\sum_{i = 0 }^n x_i = 1 \; and \;
                    \\forall i . x_i \geq 0
                    \\}
                    \\subset \mathbb{R}^{n+1}
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n+1}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              induces from the canonical topology in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n+1}
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
              this is the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^0 = *
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
              this is the standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^1 = [0,1]
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
              this is the filled triangle.
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
              this is the filled tetrahedron.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
              between topological spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              is a
              = succeed ":" do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            %p
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
              of the underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
              of every open set of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is an open set of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Topological spaces with continuous maps between them form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          #FaceInclusionInBarycentricCoords.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \n \geq 1
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi k
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \leq n
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                th
                = succeed "-face" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
              of the topological
              = succeed "-simplex," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalSimplex"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta_k : \Delta^{n-1} \hookrightarrow \Delta^n
            %p
              induced under the coordinate presentation of def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalSimplex"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^n \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{n+1}
            %p
              which “omits” the
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (x_0, \cdots , x_{n-1}) \mapsto (x_0, \cdots, x_{k-1} , 0 , x_{k}, \cdots, x_{n-1})
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p The inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta_0 : \Delta^0 \to \Delta^1
            %p is the inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{1\} \hookrightarrow [0,1]
            %p of the “right” end of the standard interval. The other inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta_1 : \Delta^0 \to \Delta^1
            %p
              is that of the “left” end
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ↪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \hookrightarrow [0,1]
          #DegeneracyProjectionsInBarycentricCoords.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \lt n
              the
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                th degenerate
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n)
              is the surjective map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sigma_k : \Delta^{n} \to \Delta^{n-1}
            %p
              induced under the barycentric coordinates of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalSimplex"}
              under the surjection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^{n+1} \to \mathbb{R}^n
            %p which sends
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (x_0, \cdots, x_n) \mapsto (x_0, \cdots, x_{k} + x_{k+1}, \cdots, x_n)
                    \\,.
          #SingularSimplex.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %strong
                singular
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
