              and we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    0
                    \\to
                    \\tilde E^\bullet(X)
                    \\stackrel{}{\longrightarrow}
                    E^\bullet(X)
                    \\stackrel{}{\longrightarrow}
                    E^\bullet(\ast)
                    \\to
                    0
            %p
              with the right map an epimorphism. Hence this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+exact+sequence"} split exact sequence
              and the statement follows.
          %h3#exmaple_ordinary_cohomology Exmaple: Ordinary cohomology
          %p (…)
          %h3#references_5 References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, section 9
            %li#Switzer75
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Switzer"}> Robert Switzer
                , chapter 7 (and 8-12) of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic Topology - Homotopy and Homology
          %h2#clutching_construction_and_product_theorem
            %strong Clutching construction and Product theorem
          %h3#idea_7 Idea
          %p
            A quick way to construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
            is to consider the trivial vector bundles on either
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hemisphere"} hemisphere
            and then glue them by a bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            on a small overlap of the two hemispheres. This is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clutching+construction"} clutching construction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+product+theorem+in+K-theory"} fundamental product theorem in K-theory
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+spaces"} product topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times S^2
            of some space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
            as that of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with one generator for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basic+line+bundle+on+the+2-sphere"} basic line bundle on the 2-sphere
            adjoined, subject to a relation. This is very useful for computations.
          %p
            Below this theorem is used to prove the all-important
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity+theorem"} Bott periodicity theorem
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
          %h3#references_6 References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, p. 17 to p. 29
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Hatcher"} Hatcher, p. 41-51
          %h2#splitting_principle
            %strong Splitting principle
          %h3#idea_8 Idea
          %p
            The second deep fact about topological K-theory is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/splitting+principle"} splitting principle
            which says roughly that as far as K-theory is concerned, all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            essentially look like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums+of+vector+bundles"} direct sums of vector bundles
            each of whose summands is just a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+operations"} Adams operations
            below. The proof of the splitting principle involves a careful analysis of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flag+spaces"} flag spaces
            in vector bundles.
          %h3#references_7 References
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, section 10
          %h2#BottPeriodicity
            %strong K-Cohomology theory
          %h3#idea_9 Idea
          %p
            Finally we may put all the pieces together and show that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-)
            indeed constitutes a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology+theory"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theory"} multiplicative cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+product+theorem+in+K-theory"} fundamental product theorem in K-theory
            implies a certain 2-periodicity in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-)
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity"} Bott periodicity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+periodic+cohomology+theory"} even periodic cohomology theory
          %p
            All these facts will be most useful for computing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X)
            and for using it to learn about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            which is what we turn to next.
          %h3#references_8 References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, p. 34 and previous
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Hatcher"} Hatcher, section 2.2
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#AguilarGitlerPrieto02"} Aguilar-Gitler-Prieto 02, appendix B
          %h2#examples_of_kgroups
            %strong Examples of K-groups
          %h3#idea_10 Idea
          %p
            In the previous sessions we have accumulated some powerful tools for computing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            groups. Here we use these to consider examples, and compute K-theory groups for a range of interesting topological spaces. This will serve to see how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            is a finer invariant than
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
          %h3#references_9 References
          %ul
            %li#Blair09
              Chris Blair,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Some K-theory examples
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.tcd.ie/~cblair/notes/kex.pdf"} pdf
          %h2#adams_operations
            %strong Adams operations
          %h3#idea_11 Idea
          %p
            So far we have used that there is the operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum+of+vector+bundles"} direct sum of vector bundles
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+bundles"} tensor product of vector bundles
            in order to exhibit the
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-)
            as taking values in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+products"} exterior products
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^n(-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-)
            inherits the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambda-ring"} Lambda-ring
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+operations"} Adams operations
            on topological K-theory. We observe their basic properties, which make them most useful for characterizing topological spaces. As a first example we see that they immediately allow to prove something as fundamental to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
          %p
            An even more interesting application of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+operations"} Adams operations
            is that they allow to characterite the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant+one"} Hopf invariant one
          %h3#references_10 References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, section 11
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Hatcher"} Hatcher, section 2.3
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#AguilarGitlerPrieto02"} Aguilar-Gitler-Prieto 02, section 10
          %h2#hopf_invariant_one
            %strong Hopf invariant one
          %h3#idea_12 Idea
          %p
            A crown jewel of the application of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+operations"} Adams operations
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant+one"} Hopf invariant one
            problem.
          %p
            A remarkable range of “exceptional” structures in mathematics is obtained by continuing the step from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            further to find the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
            and then the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            they induce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            between
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibrations"} Hopf fibrations
          %p
            These Hopf fibrations happen to take value +1 in a certain invariant, called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant"} Hopf invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant+one"} Hopf invariant one
            theorem says that they there is in fact no other fibration with this property. In particular this implies, remarkably, that the real numbers, complex numbers, quaternions and octonions are the only real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            that exist.
          %h3#references_11 References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Hatcher"} Hatcher, section 2.3
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, section 12
            %li#AguilarGitlerPrieto02
              %p
                Marcelo Aguilar,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Gitler"} Samuel Gitler
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic topology from a homotopical viewpoint
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://tocs.ulb.tu-darmstadt.de/106999419.pdf"} toc pdf
          %h2#equivariant_ktheory
            %strong Equivariant K-theory
          %h3#idea_13 Idea
          %p
            We saw how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            arises from pairing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            obtained by refining these ingredients. In particular, if instead of plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            we start by considering vector spaces equipped with linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representations"} linear representations
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            which are themselves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acted
            on by groups, and the resulting equivariant K-theory groups encode interesting information for instance about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            loci of these group actions.
          %h3#references_12 References
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Wirthmuller12"} Wirthmuller 12, section 14
          %h2#TheSpectrumKU
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\star)
            %strong The K-theory spectrum
          %p
            This section goes beyond an introduction, in that it requires more tools from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %h3#idea_14 Idea
          %p
            While we constructed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            originally for plain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+vector+bundles"} virtual vector bundles
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            constitutes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequences+in+cohomology"} long exact sequences in cohomology
            now implies that also these classifying spaces arrange in certain sequences. Suchsequences are called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
            or just
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
          %p
            The main point in its construction is the incarnation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity"} Bott periodicity
            in terms of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
          %p The relevant background for this section is laid out in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P"} Introduction to stable homotopy theory – Classical homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1-1"} Introduction to stable homotopy theory – Sequential Spectra
          %p
            The main point is that in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            (as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ClassifyingSpaces"} above
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U \times \mathbb{Z}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^0
            and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^1
          %p
            the phenomenon of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity"} Bott periodicity
            (as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BottPeriodicity"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi whe
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (B U) \times \mathbb{Z} \overset{\simeq_{whe}}{\longrightarrow} \Omega U
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi whe
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U \overset{\simeq_{whe}}{\longrightarrow} \Omega((B U) \times \mathbb{Z})
          %p
            The last one is immediate, but the first one requires work, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AguilarGitlerPrieto02"} Aguilar-Gitler-Prieto 02
          %p
            Together these show that the sequence of spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi KU
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ×
                            %mi ℤ
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi n
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mtext even
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi n
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mtext odd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  KU_n \coloneqq
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  (B U) \times \mathbb{Z} &amp; \vert n\;\text{even}
                  \\\
                  U &amp; \vert n \; \text{odd}
                  }
                  \\right.
          %p
            and equipped with the above comparison maps forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectrum"} sequential spectrum
            which is in fact an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Omega-spectrum"} Omega-spectrum
          %p
            Under the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^\bullet(-)
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory, as
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GeneralizedCohomology"} above
